==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|mīn}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*mīnaz}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/maɪ̯n/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-mein.ogg|audio|lang=de}}
* {{rhymes|aɪ̯n|lang=de}}
* {{homophone|meinen|lang=de}} {{a|according to a common pronunciation of this form}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|de|pronoun}}

# {{lb|de|possessive}} [[my]]

====Inflection====
{{de-decl-pronoun<!--
-->|mein|meine|mein|meine<!--
-->|meines|meiner|meines|meiner<!--
-->|meinem|meiner|meinem|meinen<!--
-->|meinen|meine|mein|meine<!--
-->}}

When used as a noun, the nominative masculine takes the form {{m|de|meiner}}, and the nominative/accusative neuter takes the form {{m|de|meines}} or {{m|de|meins}}.
* dein Vater und '''meiner''' – your father and mine
* dein Kind und '''mein'''(e)'''s''' – your child and mine
When the pronoun is placed after a noun, it is left uninflected:
* Gen Himmel zu dem Vater '''mein''' fahr ich von diesem Leben (song ''Nun freut euch, lieben Christen g'mein'') – Towards Heaven to my father go I from this life.

